kings and emperors historically led  armies into battle  but as you'll know heads of state  haven't exactly done that for a while  lbj wasn't fighting in vietnam and queen  elizabeth wasn't engaged in the  falklands  but when and why did this change occur  when did king's emperors and president  stop leading their troops into battle  now there are numerous reasons why kings  and emperors would so often lead armies  in the classical and medieval periods  there was an expectance that a ruler  would also be a great general and that a  king would want to win great victories  for themselves  however the main reason was simple for  the overwhelming majority of rulers  power at this point was derived from  their armies and so giving said means to  power to someone else many of whom would  have been men of rank or even close  relatives to the ruler was not a smart  idea  now even when these kings and emperors  led their soldiers into battle they  weren't  often at the fall they would mostly  stand on a hill with a good view and  give orders  this wasn't a risk-free strategy though  and whilst rare kings did get captured  and killed  so when did things change and why well a  common idea is that king stopped leading  troops after the invention of firearms  since standing on a hill behind the  action wasn't so safe  but really that wasn't the reason since  the effective range of a musket was  pretty short and so leaders were still  safe  although when firearms did become more  accurate it certainly prevented monarchs  from coming back  in fact the declining king's leading  armies occurred in the 18th century and  by the mid-19th century the practice was  pretty much dead  for example the last british monarch to  lead troops into battle was george ii in  1743 at the battle of dettingen  in fact from this point onwards most  rulers barring napoleon the first did  very little actual leading and were  essentially mascots  and obviously there are exceptions to  all of this such as in 1934 when the  king of saudi arabia led troops into  battle against yemen  so what were the reasons for this to end  well a lot of it was due to the  professionalisation  of militaries that came about after the  30 years war this centuries-long process  saw the creation of a full-time officers  corps who were ideally promoted based  upon merit  and frankly king's presidents and  emperors weren't the best at leading  armies unless they had been military  leaders before becoming heads of state  furthermore the role of these leaders  and the political landscapes they worked  in were much more stable  george iii giving the duke of wellington  a large army wasn't a threat to his  power because succession laws were  pretty solid by this point  i mean it's not like these troops would  have declared him king after his victory  at waterloo was tended to happen in say  the roman empire and the same applied to  other rulers james madison wasn't under  threat of any military coup when  washington was sacked since the founding  values of the us government were well  respected  as the centuries progressed wars became  much greater in scale  gone were the days of a single ruler  leading one army across a country  instead it was done by a general staff  who had spent their lives as soldiers  and could understand the complexities of  war in a way that a professional rubber  stamp could not  and also culture no longer required for  a king or ruler to be a great military  leader  instead they just needed to be competent  in navigating internal politics and it's  these reasons the professionalization of  the military the expansion of war scale  and complexity the security of modern  constitutions and cultural change which  said that the ruler no longer needed to  be a warrior which saw a king's role on  the battlefield come to an end  i hope you enjoyed this episode and a  special thanks to my patrons  james bissonette the pastry section  danny maloney marvin castell kelly  moneymaker rob waterhouse john bisquez  mo james castaneda aaron the white  jordan longley marcus asner gustav swann  jerry lamdin john bailey calling  castleman rasheed ali spinning three  plates field oink oink maggie patzkowski  david silverman izzy  lexi schwinn robert wetzel skye  chappelle spencer lightfoot and winston  k  wood lizzy would have been great at it  though
 

